[h1]Heroes Evolve[/h1]

Lords in Bannerlord are handed an equipment set when they come of age, and then
nothing ever happens to it. Some are handed a broken one -- a single weapon, or
civilian clothes to fight a war in. The rest keep whatever they were given at
eighteen until the day they die, while their skills sit still beside it.

This mod gives them a career instead. It repairs the broken ones, lets all of
them improve at what they do, and sends them to the market to spend their own
clan's money on their own gear. Nothing is granted for free that a lord could
buy, and nothing is bought that his skills do not justify.

[h2]What it does[/h2]

[b]Repairs lords the come-of-age bug left half-equipped.[/b] Too few weapons to
fight with, or still in civilian clothing. They are given a basic kit chosen for
their skills -- never the best one. Repair decides what kind of fighter you are;
buying decides how good you are.

[b]Grows their skills for life.[/b] One to three points a year in a skill he
actually uses -- the weapons he carries, and the trades he has put focus into --
aimed at a ceiling set by his own talent. He closes a share of the remaining gap
every year, so he climbs all his life and never quite arrives. A born horse
archer will outgrow a mediocre one and never become a champion duellist.

[b]Sends them shopping.[/b] A lord entering a town buys what he can afford
there, from that town's actual stock and with his clan's actual money. He still
improves gradually, but for the honest reason: no town stocks everything, his
purse does not stretch, and his skill decides what he is allowed to wear at all.
Wealth matters, because a great house outfits its lords better than a poor one.
Money is spent, not conjured -- and never the gold his troops are owed.

[b]He only ever improves what he is already carrying.[/b] This is the promise
the whole mod is built on. Shopping never fills an empty slot, never changes
what kind of fighter he is, and never puts a man on a horse who was fighting on
foot. A swordsman looks for a better sword; he does not come back with a bow. A
lord riding a rouncey may buy a better mount, but a lord on foot stays on foot.
Whatever he is carrying is what gets better -- whether this mod's repair handed
it to him, or you did.

[b]Lets a captor rob his prisoner.[/b] By character, not by dice roll. His word
binds him hardest, then his loyalty to his own and his pity equally: a merciful,
generous, honourable lord will not strip a beaten man, and a dishonourable one
usually will. Bandits always do.

Measured across a campaign of four hundred lords, at the shipped setting: the
median robs about one prisoner in sixteen, the most honourable about one in a
hundred, the worst about one in four. Across the whole map that comes to roughly
one lord in nine stripped in a year -- enough that it shapes who is well armed
and who is starting again, and not so much that it decides it. Both ends of that
are a slider.

[b]And the gear stays in the world.[/b] Nothing taken is destroyed. It goes into
the captor's baggage, where he may wear it, sell it in a town, or lose it when
somebody beats him -- including you. Beat the lord who robbed you and your own
armour is there on the loot screen, because it is genuinely there. It is the
game's own loot system doing it, not a special case.

[h2]The same rules for everyone[/h2]

You are not exempt and you are not privileged. Lords rob your prisoners on the
same terms you rob theirs -- except that nothing ever decides for you. You rob
by asking, in conversation, and the man answers you. A lord with no honour left
to offend says so. A friend says he expected it from anyone but you.

That includes your own house. Your family, and the companions you have given a
party or a caravan to, are lords the AI takes into battle, so they are repaired,
they grow, and they go shopping like anyone else. If you would rather outfit
them yourself, one switch moves that line around your whole clan.

Gear is where the line falls, never learning. The heroes riding in your own
party keep the equipment you gave them -- that inventory is yours, and a mod
tidying it would be taking something away -- but they go on improving like
everyone else, so a companion who has been at your side for twenty years does
not quietly fall behind the map. Nothing can overshoot: a hero already at the
ceiling his talent sets receives nothing, so this only ever catches up someone
who has fallen behind.

[h2]Configuration[/h2]

Sixteen settings in the in-game options screen if you have Mod Configuration
Menu, and in [i]settings.xml[/i] if you do not. MCM is optional, not required.
Every setting takes effect immediately -- no restart, no reload.

You can turn off any of the four systems independently, exempt your own clan,
move the gear ceiling,
change how freely lords spend, scale or disable the robbery, and strike specific
item ids from what lords are allowed to buy. There is also a one-click census
that writes a full survey of every lord in your campaign to a log file -- the
same survey every number quoted here was measured with, so you can check them
against your own game rather than taking them on trust.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]No Harmony patches.[/b] Nothing in the game engine is rewritten.
[*][b]Writes nothing into your save.[/b] The mod keeps no data of its own.
[*][b]Safe to add or remove mid-campaign.[/b] Add it and lords start improving
from wherever they are. Remove it and the campaign carries on -- though lords
keep the gear they bought or were given, the same way they would keep anything
else they own.
[*]Works with mods that add items: new gear is simply part of what lords may
buy, within the same rules. Incendiary weapons are refused outright, and you can
exclude anything else by id.
[*][b]War Sails is not required, and is what this was developed on.[/b] Every
figure quoted above was measured on campaigns running the naval DLC, so it is
tested with it rather than merely expected to survive it. It depends on none of
it and runs the same without.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]

[list]
[*]Mount & Blade II: Bannerlord [b]v1.4.8[/b]
[*][i]Optional:[/i] Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) v5, for the in-game settings screen
[/list]

[h2]Languages[/h2]

English, Spanish, French, German, Russian.

[h2]Installation[/h2]

Extract into your [i]Modules[/i] folder and enable "Heroes Evolve" in the
launcher. Load it after MCM if you use it.

[h2]Notes[/h2]

Nearly every number in this mod was measured on live campaigns rather than
guessed, and several were corrected when the measurements disagreed with the
design. The census exists so you can do the same on your own campaign.

Bug reports are welcome. Turn on logging, reproduce the problem, and send
[i]hev.log[/i] -- it is in the Bannerlord logs folder and it will usually say
exactly what happened.
